RECODE OF REGISTER ALLOCATION

The present register allocator tracks registers from loads to stores, and 
handles overflow issues by issuing pushes and pops according to the tree
structure of the intermediate code.

Any code generator must start with a general model and optimize it by improving
specific cases. That is, it must be able to handle any code case, no matter how
bad. This would create code that can handle any case, but is expensive for most
common cases. So adding optimization for general cases "fixes" the general case.

The new allocator sees loads and stores as "requests" from the code and handles
them as asyncronous from the intermediate tree. Each register has its contents
marked according to the cases:

1. There is no content (register is free).

2. There is a simple content. The register contains a simple, loadable type.

3. The register contains a complex type that is only reproducable by 
calculation.

4. The register is free, but incidently contains a simple type or its address.

5. There is a simple content in a register, but it is marked as "stored" or
"done".

Another possibility is that a register contains a free, but complex type. Reuse
of such subexpressions is left to a higher level intermediate pass.

The allocator processes the code without regard for the tree structure of the
intermediate. The actions are:

Load

A variable, or its address, is loaded. The allocator considers all register
contents for a previous such element. If it is found, and free, it is used.
If it is found, but not free (in use by another intermediate), it is copied.
If no previous contents are found, then the register allocation process is
entered.

Store

The register is marked free.

Mark content

The Marks the contents of a register as complex.

Join context

Combines multiple flows into the current register context.

Purge context

Dump all active registers (registers with simple, storable context).

When a register is allocated, we search for a free register. If there is no
completely free register, then we choose one that is free, but contains
content, based on the age of that entry.

If none of the registers qualifies, then a storeable register is choosen.
These are registers that were marked stored, but the store has not taken place.
The store must be executed, then the register used.

Finally, if all these steps fail, then the expression tree is sufficiently
complex that the register set cannot contain it all. This means that a complex
entry must be spilled to a temporary. A temp is created as a local, then the
register is spilled to that. The code that requests that item will cause it
to be reloaded.

Processors like the x86 series are relatively unaffected by spills and reloads,
because they can represent full loads and stores to locals without occupying
multiple registers. However, use of push and pops may decrease the expense on
these processors, and on processors that have potentially expensive locals
access, push and pop processing can dramatically lower the expense.

For this reason, A second pass is to look through each basic block examining
which items can be spilled and reloaded by attaching push/pop operations to
the code.

Finally, a "load bridging" pass is completed that tries to bring old register
contents forward by copying them to side registers.

